 210: def main(grid: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray:
 211:     """
 212:     Extend each seed diagonally into its matching frame and let crossed objects recolor the later ray segments.
 213: 
 214:     Input structure:
 215:     - The grid background is black.
 216:     - Several non-overlapping one-pixel-thick rectangular frames appear in distinct colors.
 217:     - Outside the frames sit singleton seed pixels whose colors match frame colors, plus a few small monochrome 2-4 pixel filter objects in other colors.
 218: 
 219:     Rule:
 220:     - Pair every seed with the unique rectangular frame of the same color.
 221:     - From each seed, follow the only diagonal direction that first reaches that matching frame, and draw the ray through the hit frame pixel.
 222:     - Start the ray in the seed color, but whenever the path crosses a filter object, recolor that crossed pixel and every later ray pixel with the object's color; later crossed objects override earlier ones.
 223:     - Erase every filter object that was crossed by a ray, then draw the recolored ray segments on top.
 224: 
 225:     Key insight:
 226:     - Color identifies which frame belongs to a seed, geometry identifies which diagonal works, and the crossed objects act as visible color filters that update the rest of the ray.
 227:     """
 228:     return _solve(np.asarray(grid, dtype=int))
